---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="A place for curiosity"
  description="TIL is a daily reading habit for curious people. One short, sourced discovery about the world, every day."
>
  <section class="about-page shell">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">TODAY I LEARNED</span>
      <h1>
        You don't have to know
        <br />
        where curiosity leads.
      </h1>
      <p>You just need somewhere to start.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        <Icon name="asterisk-simple" />
        <span>STAY CURIOUS.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>A few minutes for something new</h2>
        <p>
          TIL is a place to learn one interesting thing each day. Something about the natural world,
          an overlooked piece of history, or an idea that makes an ordinary moment a little more
          interesting.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each edition starts with a question and gives you enough context to understand the answer.
          If it catches your attention, the sources are there to take you further.
        </p>
        <h2>How stories get here</h2>
        <p>
          Topics are chosen and prepared in advance. Everyone sees the same daily edition. We aim to
          publish each morning at 06:15 India Standard Time. If we miss a day, the latest story
          stays here with its original date.
        </p>
        <h2>Sources, with room for uncertainty</h2>
        <p>
          We start with research institutions, museums, original studies, and other sources with
          relevant expertise. Every edition links to the material behind it. A proposed explanation
          stays a proposed explanation; a good story shouldn't need a stronger claim than the
          evidence supports.
        </p>
        <h2>Words that earn their place</h2>
        <p>
          We review copy for clear explanations and natural language. The editing pass removes
          filler and checks that a rewrite hasn't changed a fact or dropped an important
          qualification.
        </p>
        <h2>When something needs correcting</h2>
        <p>
          Corrections belong in the article. When we change a factual claim, we add a dated
          correction note explaining what changed. Small spelling and formatting fixes don't need a
          note.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you spot a problem, open an issue in this site's source repository if you have access,
          including the article link and a source we can check.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/">
          Read the latest discovery <Icon name="arrow-right" />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
